[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m setters, no handle/context object and no state to initialise** — the library is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a single pure function, so the "options" axis is carried entirely by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `lsbit` mode argument and the "input shape" axes by the value classes below.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Entry points (full set — no wrappers omitted)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | entry point | level | notes |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `encode_quant(uni, step, pred, tgt, tgt2, lsbit)` | **lowest level == only level** | The library exports exactly one symbol (see `SYMBOLS.md`). There is no convenience / one-shot wrapper above it and no internal helper below it, so "exercise the low-level entry points directly" is satisfied by construction. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Axes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **L — `lsbit` mode** (the one runtime option; dispatch is exhaustive over all `int`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `L0`  : `lsbit == 0` → no LSB forcing at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `L4`  : `lsbit == 4` → clear bit0, then `x |= (x>>1) & (x>>2) & 1` on all three candidates. Checked **before** `lsbit & 1`, so `4` does *not* take the even branch.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `LODD`: `lsbit != 0,4` and `lsbit & 1` → force bit0 **set** on all three candidates (includes negative odd).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `LEVEN`: `lsbit != 0,4` and `!(lsbit & 1)` → force bit0 **clear** (includes `-4`, `INT_MIN`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **K — candidate-clamp shape**, driven by `uni & 7` (mutually exclusive; "both clamped" is unreachable):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `K0`  : `uni & 7 == 0` → `uni-1` borrows past bit 2, so `uni2` is clamped to `uni`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `K7`  : `uni & 7 == 7` → `uni+1` carries past bit 2, so `uni1` is clamped to `uni`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   * `KMID`: `uni & 7 ∈ 1..=6` → neither candidate clamped.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **G — `uni & 8`** (`S0` / `S8`): selects whether `diff` is negated. Bit 3 is identical across `uni`/`uni1`/`uni2` after clamping (clamp guarantees it, and `L*` only touches bit 0), so this is a single shared axis.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **T — `step` class**: `0`, small positive, small negative, magnitude large enough to overflow `(2*(uni&7)+1)*step` (`> INT_MAX/15`), `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **W — winner selection** (`d0` kept / `d1` wins / `d2` wins / both `d1<d0` and `d2<d0` / exact ties): both comparisons are against the **original** `d0`, and `d2` is tested last, so when both beat `d0` the result is `uni2` even if `d1 < d2`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **V — value shape of `pred`/`tgt`/`tgt2`**: near-zero, `tgt2 == tgt`, `tgt2` far from `tgt` (so the `d3 >> 5` tiebreak term dominates), and `i32` extremes that wrap.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **N — sign of `uni`**: negative `uni` makes `uni >> 1` / `uni >> 2` in the `L4` branch arithmetic (sign-propagating) shifts.
